  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Of 15 doors checked against the official wording, 5 need Biology, 6 do not, and 4 depend on your state or college. A PCM student keeps more options than most pages admit.</strong> The biggest closed door, medicine, can be reopened by adding Biology as an extra subject.
    Pick by the written rule and build a high-value skill portfolio beside the degree, and you reach better income options and earlier financial freedom. Pick by a listicle and you can pay for a form that never applied to you.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Named seat pools open without Biology total 75,748 (JoSAA 67,323, ICAR about 6,002, IISER 2,343, NFSU 80). NEET-linked seats total 2,22,827. The Biology-free pools are about one third the size, but the contest per seat is far lighter.</li>
      <li>An NMC public notice of 22 November 2023 lets Biology or Biotechnology be added as an additional subject after Class 12. Fees can be as low as Rs 1,220. The hard part is NEET itself, where Biology is 360 of 720 marks.</li>
      <li><strong>Your next step: choose two doors from the audit table, open each official notice and highlight the Class 12 subject line. Then build one small proof piece in the lane you lean toward.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Every rule below comes from a notice, a bulletin or a counselling report, and the sources are listed at the end. For the wider map, see <a href="/blog/career-options/">our career options guides</a>. If you want a person to test your shortlist against your marks, state and budget, <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">continuous career guidance</a> builds a high-value skill portfolio around your shortlist, and the <a href="/career-skills-compass/">Career &amp; Skills Compass</a> is a quick first read on your strengths.
  </p>

