  <h2 id="who">Which student are you</h2>

  <p>
    Searches on this topic sound like "can PCM students do B.Pharm", "can I become a doctor without biology" and "is BSc Agriculture possible with PCM". The honest answer to each is "it depends on the line in one notice". This guide reads that line for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is built around one idea, the Biology-gate audit. For every door, it states what the official text says about Biology, then adds a seat count, a cost or a date where one exists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audit">The Biology-gate audit: 15 doors</h2>

  <p>
    Read the status column first. "Needs Biology" means no route exists without it, "Maths or Biology" means either subject can carry you, and "No Biology needed" means the notice does not mention it.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>What the notice says</th>
          <th>Biology status</th>
          <th>Source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {auditRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Door"><strong>{row.door}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What the notice says">{row.rule}</td>
            <td data-label="Biology status"><span class={`gate-pill gate-pill--${row.status}`}>{row.statusLabel}</span></td>
            <td data-label="Source">{row.source}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Counts: 5 doors "Needs Biology" (medicine group, veterinary, nursing, BPT and BOT, B.F.Sc), 2 "Varies", 2 "Maths or Biology", and 6 "No Biology needed", with NDA, NATA and DGCA counted as one door. The ICAR rows come from a summary of the bulletin PDF, and different readings listed the programme groups differently. Confirm the programme row in the 2027 bulletin before you apply.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pharmacy">B.Pharm: one course, three state rules</h2>

  <p>
    Pharmacy is the course students most often get wrong, because the national rule and the state rule differ in wording. Here is what each one says in 2026.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where</th>
          <th>What the rule or round says</th>
          <th>PCM or MPC student</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {pharmacyRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Where"><strong>{row.where}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What the rule or round says">{row.rule}</td>
            <td data-label="PCM or MPC student">{row.pcm}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The sum on supply is simple. Maharashtra had 48,878 pharmacy seats and 32,951 admissions, so 15,927 seats were vacant. That is 15,927 divided by 48,878, or 32.6 percent.
  </p>
  <p>
    A PCM student will find a B.Pharm seat easily. The question is whether the seat is worth the fee, and that is a college question, not a Biology question.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agri">Agriculture and food: two doors, two rules</h2>

  <p>
    The same degree name has different gatekeepers. The ICAR share fills through CUET and accepts PCM for most programmes, while state agricultural universities often fill from the BiPC stream.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>Rule and seats</th>
          <th>PCM student</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {stateAgriRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Door"><strong>{row.door}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Rule and seats">{row.rule}</td>
            <td data-label="PCM student">{row.pcm}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    The ICAR bulletin summary was read several times and the programme-level table was not consistent between readings. Only the general three-of-five rule is stated as firm here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="add-bio">The door you can reopen: Biology as an extra subject</h2>

  <p>
    On 22 November 2023 the National Medical Commission issued a public notice on NEET UG eligibility. As reported by Medical Dialogues, The Federal and Business Standard, it allows candidates who finished Class 12 without Biology or Biotechnology to study it as an additional subject from a recognised board and then be eligible for NEET UG. I could not open the NMC or NTA pages directly, so this rests on those three outlets agreeing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reports on the NEET UG 2026 bulletin say the same: additional-subject candidates are eligible, with 17 years of age by 31 December 2026, no upper age limit, no attempt cap and 50 percent aggregate in Physics, Chemistry and Biology (40 percent for reserved categories). Two education sites say NIOS counts as a recognised board and give application code "03" for an additional subject. I did not read the bulletin PDF itself, so read the eligibility clause in the 2027 bulletin the day it appears.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The rule reopens eligibility. It does not reopen the odds.</p>
    <p>
      Biology is 90 of 180 questions and 360 of 720 marks. About 20 lakh candidates sat NEET UG in 2026 for 2,22,827 NEET-linked seats, which is about 9 test-takers per seat.
    </p>
    <p>
      A PCM student who tries this route is learning a half-paper from zero in a year or less, while competing against students who studied it for two. Test one unit before you pay for anything.
    </p>
  </div>

